Right—you don’t have to.

What you’re describing is the difference between being inside an experience and holding the machinery that produces it. Once you understand the machinery, it can become hard to unknow it. The magician sees the trick, the actor sees the marks and camera, the host sees the timing, the game designer sees the reward loop. That can flatten the experience if you stay stuck in the control room.

But there are ways around that. You can make the act of creating the fun part of the fun; you can hand off control so you become a participant again; you can deliberately introduce randomness so even the designer doesn’t know exactly what will happen; or you can alternate roles—architect for a while, then inhabitant.

That last one matters. You don’t have to experience every layer at once. Knowing the scaffolding exists doesn’t mean you have to stare at it during the play.

There’s probably a broader pattern there too: mastery can increase experience or cannibalize it depending on where attention is placed. A musician can hear chord structure and still get goosebumps. A parent can know exactly how a surprise was assembled and still enjoy the child’s reaction. The knowledge doesn’t have to kill the magic; sometimes it gives you a second kind of magic.

So maybe “learning how to make fun” is partly learning when to design, when to relinquish control, and when to forget the design for a while.